{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792630000000-reviewer-8ab-descriptors.claude-fable-5-1.stdout.log\"","aggregated_output":"[claude-code:unrecognized_model] {\"model\":\"muse-spark-1.3-contributor\",\"query_source\":\"sdk\"}\nVerification complete. All eight brief items checked against repository evidence; two blocking findings. Delivering the review:\n\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-10-14 09:40:00 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-14 16:40:00 +07\nCoding-Agent: claude\nSession-ID: 71ff60b2-6c20-4e6c-9a77-6cfced87ce56\n\n# Phase-8ab descriptor + BLAKE3 review: `67b98907..b794e2f5` (16 tracked files)\n\n**Merge verdict: NOT ready to merge into `srvm-phase8`.** Two blocking findings: one in-scope P1 regression (a provably red test the slice should have regenerated), one P2 in claimed acceptance (the 3-host gate). Everything else in the slice verifies clean against repo evidence — no new architectural defect found, and the report's deviations all check out. Fixes are small (<1 day).\n\n## Findings\n\n**P1 | `test/resources/yang/python/dht-import-v1.edn:2,6` | evidence | concrete fix**\nThe artifact still pins the OLD py manifest `:segment/blake3-b9dbaeff80b3…` (line 2) and the OLD signed envelopes over OLD manifest addresses (line 6: `pym.m` `eef33c8c…`, `pym.k` `1221aa01…`, `pym.t` `5d12f25c…`), while this slice moved py to `577aabfb8632…` (proven by `linked_prelude_test.cljc` golden change in this same diff) and every H/R embedded in the guest blobs. Two assertions in the untouched consumer must now fail: `dht_import_test.cljc:370-378` (`this-host-publishes-the-artifact` — this host's `fixture/generate` vs the committed bytes) and `dht_import_test.cljc:397-398` (`(= (:py artifact) (:address @h/published))`). The repo itself prescribes the fix: `dht-import-v1.generate.clj:19-20` says \"Rerun it whenever the prelude, the lowering or the linker's publication moves an address; `dht-import-test` fails until it is.\" The engineer brief item (4) explicitly scoped \"any manifest/fixture golden that embeds an H or R\" into 8ab, and the old py address is in the report's own table — a grep for it would have found this file. The miss is contained: I grepped the full new tree for all old H/R/manifest prefixes (`52791d4a`, `c0aefe2f`, `e971cd9b`, `afe9e3ac`, `b9dbaeff`, `0971cc1e`) and this edn is the sole leftover. **Fix:** run the generator on the JVM per its header (`clojure … -M:t test/resources/yang/python/dht-import-v1.generate.clj`, local-only, no network — committed packets + fixed seeds), commit the rewritten edn in this slice, and run `dht_import_test` including the slow mesh leg (needs real loopback, cf. the report's socket note).\n\n**P2 | (verification gap — no single line; claimed acceptance) | evidence | concrete fix**\nNo Node or Dart lane ran: report Verification table states \"no commit, Node lane or Dart lane was run.\" Cross-host agreement of the new BLAKE3 pins is therefore unproven, and it is claimed acceptance three ways: engineer brief \"Portability: `.cljc` for JVM/Node/Dart\"; `stack_parity_test.cljc:14-17` (\"committed literals asserted on every host lane, which is B5's cross-runtime identity check\"); architect §3 gate list (\"three hosts at every landing from 8ab up\"). Mitigating evidence I verified: `jing/blake3` has explicit per-host implementations (`dao/jing.cljc:114-135`, `:clj`/`:cljs`/`:cljd`) and BLAKE3 was already the Jing default exercised on all hosts — so residual risk is narrow (host-specific encoder divergence, e.g. the known CLJS double-provenance split the parity suite already branches on). **Fix:** run Node + Dart quick lanes over the nine touched test namespaces plus the `handoff-v2-census-test` before merging to `srvm-phase8`; after the P1 regen, run the `dht-import` slow test on all three hosts (its docstring at `dht_import_test.cljc:9-21` requires every host to arrive at the JVM artifact).\n\n**P3 | `test/yin/vm/debruijn/stack_parity_test.cljc:169` | evidence | concrete fix**\nTest message still reads \"adapt + stack VM agrees with lower + semantic VM\" but pipeline A is now `derive/stack-image` (`image-of` at :94-96, `debruijn-outcome` docstring at :142 corrected). Cosmetic only. **Fix:** reword to \"derive + stack VM agrees …\".\n\n**P3 | `docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.derivations.md:1092,1094` | evidence | concrete fix**\nThe §8.1 table still cites the pre-8ab H/R prefixes (`52791d4a…`, `c0aefe2f…`) and `dl/adapt`/`rc/adapt` helper refs as the live check, and \"the twelve R hashes\" where the code has 11 (implementer already reported the inventory error). The mechanical-check prose remains valid, so this is doc staleness only. **Fix:** acceptable in the 8g docs sweep — annotate 8ab completion with the new prefixes and correct \"twelve\" → \"eleven\".\n\n## The eight brief items, with evidence\n\n1. **Two-row descriptor change, versions pinned — CONFIRMED.** HEAD vs new forms compared directly: H descriptor drops `[:… :dim/lift-to [:yin.code/*]]` and flips `:hash :sha256→:blake3` (`debruijn_code.cljc:128-141`); R descriptor identical shape of change (`debruijn_register_code.cljc:196-…`). `lowering-contract-version` is still `2` (`debruijn_code.cljc:109-114`, value line untouched) and `contract-version` still `4` (value untouched; only its docstring now cites ruling 2). (`debruijn.cljc`'s own descriptor correctly keeps `:dim/lift-from`/`projection-to` — only its `:dim/encoding` row changed, `:hash` plus the `:node-hash` prose, which is the Q4 second-row change, not a lift row.)\n2. **Six BLAKE3 sites, no SHA-256 path — CONFIRMED.** `jing/blake3` at `debruijn_code.cljc:542,558`, `debruijn_register_code.cljc:258,270`, `debruijn.cljc:966,1028`; `:hash :blake3` rows at `debruijn_code.cljc:133`, `debruijn_register_code.cljc:201`, `debruijn.cljc:139`; docstrings rewritten (H/R/node formulas). `grep -rn sha256|SHA-256` over all three source files returns nothing; calls are plain (no reader conditionals) and `jing/blake3` dispatches per host (`dao/jing.cljc:128-135`). SHA-256 correctly remains for the DHT routing contract (classification doc) and the Jing registry.\n3. **Regeneration through public functions — CONFIRMED by 5 end-to-end spot checks** (brief allowed mental rerun + spot checks; I did not execute per the read-only instruction). Each recompute fn verified public (`defn`, not `defn-`): `dc/image-hash` (:545), `rcode/register-hash` (:261), `jing/blake3` (jing:128), `dc/encode-scalar` (:418), `db/encode-value` (debruijn.cljc:902), `db/project-datoms` (:1103), `vm/ast->datoms` (vm.cljc:882), `jing/segment-key` (jing:182). Checked: H-descriptor `44c761ee…→7b7c6ae9…` pins in `debruijn_code_test`; dimension `1f4ec95c…→4b69e00d…` pins in `debruijn_test`; linker H `52791d4a…→d07cb2e9…`; py manifest `b9dbaeff…→577aabfb…` from the full printed manifest map; handoff `afe9e3ac…→52c7efaa…` plus the regen script's exact-CBOR-bytes assertion (`regen.clj:35-40`), with the script's descriptor-equality asserts (:25-31). Derive-test is 10H+10R (not the plan's 11 — plan-inventory error, code confirmed 10+10 in the diff); the 4 extra `debruijn_code_test` goldens, 4 parity rows with wire bytes kept, and linker goldens via `derive` all match table rows. The 51-row table matches the 51-entry claim (50 distinct new values; linker-R `bc069145…` correctly equals the new lowerer's worked-example R — different from the old allocator's `70de2f46…`, as it should be). The byte-identical-rerun claim itself is unverified by execution, but the script is deterministic by inspection (`sort-by key`, pure recompute, no time/net/randomness).\n4. **No A address moved — CONFIRMED.** `git diff --quiet` passes for `corpus.cljc`, `rows_test.cljc`, `layout_test.cljc`, `rows-checkpoint-v3.txt`, `ledger-v2.txt`; the safepoint harness is absent from the 16-file diff. This satisfies the unitarity invariant (`datom.world.md:24-32`: the AST is never lost; derived forms are reinterpretations) as operationalized for phase 8 — the changed segment addresses (py/pysp/handoff) are content addresses whose *content* embeds regenerated H/R, not moved identities.\n5. **Kernels intact, `encode-scalar` in place — CONFIRMED.** `stack.cljc`/`register.cljc` absent from the diff; `encode-scalar` still `defn` at `debruijn_code.cljc:418`, still referenced by `debruijn_register_code` (`:scalar-encoder` data ref + encoder calls) and still tested in place. No widening, no removal, no dangling reference — the report's hazard analysis is correct.\n6. **C4 goldens correctly derived; 8c regen dependency REAL — CONFIRMED.** Both spot-checked end-to-end (item 3). The 8ab manifests still carry `:yin.semantic/code \"v3\"` and the old `{H addr R addr}` index shape (intermediates py entry), while `derivations.md:1095` documents the 8c change to `\"v4\"` plus the `{:yin.debruijn.code … :yin.debruijn.register …}` index reshape — so 8c necessarily regenerates them again, exactly as the report claims.\n7. **Handoff fixture — CONFIRMED.** The 18+/18- diff is the address line plus six hunks swapping the 64-char ASCII H (`e971cd9b…`→`688b2b45…`) inside CBOR text fields; no other bytes change. The plan's \"no H/R in old fixtures\" was wrong for this file (raw-hex grep misses ASCII-in-CBOR) and the report's explanation is accurate. Fixture still present, dies in 8d per plan. No test pins the old address (grep: zero hits).\n8. **Old compiler test — CONFIRMED as coverage-preserving.** 11 pins regenerated (counted in diff), requires and the `(adapted …)` producer untouched (no require hunk), duplicated descriptor golden deleted. Same assertions, new hashes, no shim — correct interim posture until the planned 8e deletion. Related: the pairing verifier staying on the old allocator is also correct — `linker_test.cljc` scopes `rc/adapt` to the local `old-register-image` in the one authentic-pairing test while all general helpers use `derive`; production `linker.cljc` is untouched (absent from diff), so no 8c semantic leaks in early.\n\n## Method limits\n\nRead-only per the brief: I ran no tests, linters, or the regen script. The report's test/lint/mutant numbers (250 quick + 17 slow focused; 1149 broad; kondo clean; 4/4 mutants caught with sound cp-diff-restore methodology) are taken as reported, except where contradicted by evidence — the P1 (a suite member outside all reported gates is provably red) and the P2 (the reported gates are JVM-only where the phase gate requires three hosts). `target/8ab/` logs live outside the worktree and were not inspected.\n\n**Bottom line:** the hash move itself is correct, complete, and invariant-preserving; the deviations are all legitimate and evidenced. Unblock by regenerating `dht-import-v1.edn` and running the Node/Dart lanes, then merge.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
